        commonMistakes: [
          `Using it for domain users. Get-LocalUser sees only accounts on this computer; use Get-ADUser for Active Directory accounts.`,
          `Asking for an account that does not exist and being surprised by an error. Get-LocalUser -Name 'bob' raises "User bob was not found" (a UserNotFoundException); add -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue in scripts, or test with wildcards.`,
          `Looking only at the default three columns. LastLogon, PasswordLastSet and PasswordExpires are the useful audit properties and are hidden until you select them.`,
        ],
        troubleshooting: `If the command is not recognised, you are on an older system without Windows PowerShell 5.1 (the LocalAccounts module comes with 5.1). On a domain controller there are no local accounts, so the list is empty or the command fails: use Get-ADUser. If LastLogon is blank, the account has not signed in yet. Reading does not require elevation, but changing accounts does.`,
        relatedCommands: [`New-LocalUser`, `Remove-LocalUser`, `Where-Object`, `Get-ADComputer`, `Format-List`],
        relatedLessonPath: `/learn/chapter-09/hands-on-admin-workout`,
        isEssentialPocketknife: false,
